<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Art of War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">5th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Art of War is an ancient Chinese treatise on strategy in 13 chapters, attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-tzu">Sun Tzu</a> and probably completed between 500 and 430 BCE. It treats war as a costly last resort, led the Seven Military Classics and has been read by commanders in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> and the West.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-art-of-war.jpg" alt="The Art of War" width="330" height="251" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: vlasta2, bluefootedbooby on flickr.com · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bamboo_book_-_unfolded_-_UCR.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its foundations are the Five Classics, among them the Book of Songs, the oldest collection of Chinese poetry, and the Book of Changes, and the works of the philosophers, among them Confucius, Mencius, Laozi, Zhuangzi, Mozi and Han Feizi, and the <b>Art of War</b> of Sunzi.</blockquote><a href="/language/classical-chinese">Classical Chinese · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mozi is highly respected by modern scholars and ranks as a classic on military matters on a par with Sunzi’s <b>Art of War</b>, the former on defensive strategy, the latter on offensive strategy.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/mohism">Mohism · In Power</a></li></ul></section>
